sixteen-year-old pets was raised by his  activist grandmother  and he didn't hesitate in joining the  student protest movement when it began  earlier this year this week he took part  in an act of civil disobedience  organized by the group  bad students many refused to wear  official school uniforms to class  this was to highlight inequality and to  call for reforms in thailand's education  system  petch had a black uniform made and  displayed rubber ducks  which has now become a symbol for the  protests  after being questioned for three hours  by teachers he was sent home  it's not only about the uniform rules  haircut rules it's about body rights  the expression of political opinions and  the education system  and subjects need to be reformed these  would be victories for us  protesters were out in big numbers on  wednesday after the constitutional court  unanimously  acquitted prime minister prey chenoucha  of violating ethics standards  ruling that it was not illegal for the  prime minister a former army chief of  staff  to live in a house on an army base while  the protesters here would have welcomed  a guilty verdict for the prime minister  they vow to carry on their fight to see  that their demands are fulfilled and  that is that the prime minister resigned  there's a new draft to the constitution  and that there's reform within the  monarchy  has been the focus of more rallies this  week first targeting his estimated 30  billion dollar fortune  thousands gathered at the headquarters  of one of the biggest banks in thailand  the king's the largest shareholder  personally owning 23 percent  and then a protest was held in front of  the royal guards barracks demanding that  the king give up his command  of two army regiments he took over when  he ascended to the throne two years ago  at the same time he also shifted the  crown's wealth to him  personally for the first time in two  years officials  issued warrants to protest leaders under  article 112  harsh defamation laws designed to  protect the monarchy  charged multiple times under that law  but never convicted sulak siwalak  has criticized action taken against  protesters  he has met with a king in the past and  says that it's key for him to engage  with the protesters  his position is not as secure as he  thought  and he tried to be popular but so far he  had not managed  to confront those who supposed to be  against him  and i feel that if he would be willing  to  confront those who declared himself to  be again a monarchy  i think that would help him but so far  the king has not  taken that advice while he has been  making public appearances  they've only been with those who support  him and the monarchy  and there's been no official palace  statement on the protests  scott heidler al-jazeera bangkok
 

